## Connecting to the server

With `host` set, every `dokku` command runs over SSH. Without it, `dokku` runs on the machine Terraform runs on, through `sudo` when `sudo` is true. SSH picks its key from ssh-agent or `~/.ssh/config` unless `ssh_identity_file` names one, in which case that key is offered alone. `accept_new_host_keys` trusts a host SSH has not seen before, and a changed host key is still refused. Commands that overlap share one SSH connection, which closes as soon as no command is using it.

## Version floors

Every task declares the oldest Dokku release it supports, and any plugins it needs. The provider refuses a task the server is too old for, on apply and on refresh, rather than reading a resource it cannot parse as missing. `ignore_version` turns the check off.

## Collections are authoritative

A resource that manages a collection, such as `dokku_config`, `dokku_domains`, `dokku_ports`, `dokku_buildpacks` or `dokku_ps_scale`, owns the whole collection for its app, service or scope. A member added outside Terraform is removed on the next apply, and leaving the collection out of the configuration declares it empty. Destroying the resource clears the collection; destroying `dokku_ps_scale` resets the app to Dokku's default formation.

## Values read from the server

An optional attribute without a default that is left out of the configuration takes the server's value, such as the image a `dokku_service_create` was created on. Removing such an attribute from the configuration later leaves the server's value in place.

Some attributes are never read back. A write-only attribute, such as a service password, is stored by the server but not returned. An apply-only attribute, such as `dokku_config`'s `restart`, steers how a change is applied and is not server state. Terraform keeps the configured value for both, and an imported resource holds them as null, or at their default, until the configuration sets them.

## Resources without a teardown

`dokku_app_clone`, `dokku_git_sync`, `dokku_git_from_archive` and `dokku_git_from_image` run an operation rather than declaring a resource. They record that the operation ran: Terraform does not read them back, changing one runs it again, and destroying one only removes it from state. The deprecated `dokku_storage_ensure` has no inverse either, so destroying it also leaves the server unchanged.

## Ordering and concurrency

Operations on the same app or service run one at a time, so resources on one app can be applied in parallel without colliding on Dokku's deploy lock. The provider does not order resources that depend on each other: reference the resource that creates an app or service, as in `app = dokku_app.api.app`, so Terraform creates it first and destroys it last. When an app or service is deleted outside Terraform, the resources that belong to it are dropped from state on refresh and created again after it.

## Import

Every resource except the ones without a teardown imports by its identity address, the same address docket uses:

```shell
terraform import dokku_config.api 'dokku_config[app=api]'
terraform import dokku_service_link.api 'dokku_service_link[app=api,service=postgres,name=db]'
```

An imported collection holds every member the server has.
